 "prologue": [
  {
   "s": "Far in the north is a small town called Kaamos: the long dark. The old people say elves lived here once, and went west over the sea. Every house still leaves one window unshuttered, in case."
  },
  {
   "s": "Ana grew up at the last fence, looking at the place where the road stops being Kaamos and becomes everywhere else. Nothing ever came down it for her."
  },
  {
   "s": "Then came the winter that did not end. A king in the west had shut the world’s light inside its great diamonds, and dropped most of them on the long way home. The sun rose thin as a coin and warmed nothing.",
   "fx": "gems"
  },
  {
   "s": "“The summer is in those stones,” said the Tonttu, who kept her house. “All of it. Somebody will have to fetch every one, and let the light out.”"
  },
  {
   "s": "Ana held out her hand, the way you would to see if it was snowing. Starlight came down into it like a bird that had been waiting for her to stand still."
  },
  {
   "s": "“Well,” said the Tonttu. “That explains the ears. Your boots are by the door. They have been by the door for years.”"
  }
 ],
 "epilogue": [
  {
   "bg": "mesa",
   "s": "Under the armour there was hardly anyone. He had only wanted to be warm."
  },
  {
   "bg": "mesa",
   "s": "The weather had eased with every stone. But the summer itself was still shut inside them. “I know a shore,” said Mayank, “where the sun goes last of all. Let it out there, and it has the whole world to cross.”"
  },
  {
   "bg": "kauai",
   "rise": true,
   "s": "So they carried the diamonds west, over one more sea, to a green island called Kauaʻi. On the sand, at the end of the day, Ana opened her hands."
  },
  {
   "bg": "dawn",
   "s": "The light went home along her road, and summer followed it. It reached Kaamos by morning. “That is the Starlight Queen,” the Tonttu tells the children. “She is from here.”"
  },
  {
   "bg": "kauai",
   "s": "“This,” said Mayank, “is the beginning of a new adventure.”"
  }
 ],
